# Epilogue: The Unwritten Future
*Looking back across the years*
And so we arrive at the end—or perhaps as Bob would say, the beginning. Because this story, like life itself, doesn't truly end. It continues, chapter after chapter, each one building on what came before, each one offering new challenges and new joys, new freedoms and new responsibilities. I've written about the shock that ignited Bobs curiosity, the childhood that shaped his passions, the education that equipped him for the journey, the career that fulfilled his ambitions, the family that gave his life meaning, the travels that expanded his horizons, the losses that taught him what's precious, the love that carried him through.
But the story doesn't stop here.
The daughter who survived aplastic anemia —she has a child of her own now. His son his grandchildren his adopted stepchildren step grandchildren and even a step great grandson. They have their own stories to write, their own chapters to add to this ongoing narrative. They will face their own electrical outlets, their own hospitals, their own cars with keys in the ignition. They will make their own choices about responsibility and freedom, about when to hold on and when to let go. The lesson Bob haas learned, the ones he hopes will echo through whatever chapters come after me, is this: life is not about avoiding the shocks. It's not about preventing every illness, avoiding every risk, shielding ourselves and our children from every possible harm.
Life is about the willingness to touch the outlet anyway. To get behind the wheel anyway. To love anyway, knowing that love always carries the possibility of loss.
